>
  <p>This is Jordan Hoppel's walk-through of how he would take someone with a 225 lb squat toward 315. If someone already squats 225, they've spent some time in the gym or have good genetics for handling training volume. He doesn't promise a timeline: it depends on training age, genetics, and how good the lifter is.</p>

  <h2>Phase 1 (weeks 1 to 4): technique first</h2>
  <p>For the first 2 to 4 weeks, make sure technique is sound and the lifter can squat to multiple depths and feel all of them.</p>

  <h2>Phase 2: high sets, low reps, slow eccentric</h2>
  <p>Then a high-set, low-rep, high-tempo progression, for example 10 sets of 6 or 10 sets of 4 with a 4 to 5 second eccentric.</p>
  <p><strong>Example, a 135 lb lifter with a 225 max:</strong> the first 4 sets are at 135 for 4 reps with a 4-second eccentric. The next 6 sets go in pairs, increasing 5 to 10 lb every two sets. Do this for the first two weeks. After that, drop to 8 sets with the same reps.</p>

  <Media type="gif" alt="Squat with a slow 4 to 5 second lowering phase" caption="A controlled 4 to 5 second eccentric." brief="GIF: slow-tempo squat, ~5 sec down, drive up." />

  <h2>Phase 3: tempo plus one-and-a-quarter squats (next 4 weeks)</h2>
  <p>Add the one-and-a-quarter squat: go all the way down to the lowest good-form depth, come up a quarter, drop back to the bottom, then all the way up. Do 8 sets of 5 for the first 2 weeks, then 6 sets of 3 for the next 2 weeks.</p>

  <h2>Phase 4: shorten the tempo</h2>
  <p>Drop the eccentric from 5 seconds to 3 seconds and keep the quarter squat for the first 2 weeks of the block. For the last 2 weeks, cut the tempo and the quarter squat, keep 8 sets, and drop reps to 2.</p>
  <p><em>Editor's note: in the interview, Jordan called this "the final 12 weeks." It may mean the final block or the whole 12-week plan. Confirm before publishing.</em></p>

  <h2>Week 13: test the squat</h2>
  <p>Jordan's mapping: going up 5 lb every set is feasible. Treat the second-to-last set as the previous week's heaviest set, and progress by one or two weight units (one unit is 5 lb, the next is 10 lb) to project where the lifter would end up after 12 weeks.</p>

  <JordanQuote>I don't know how long it will take. It depends on training age, genetics, and how good they are.</JordanQuote>

  <p>If your squat is stuck for other reasons, start with <a href="/why-your-squat-stopped-going-up/">why your squat stopped going up</a>.</p>
</GuideLayout>
